.\" fflush.3 - flush a stream
.TH FFLUSH 3 "2026-05-30" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fflush \- flush a stream
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "int fflush(FILE *" stream ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
For output streams,
.BR fflush ()
forces a write of all user-space buffered data for the given output or update
.I stream
via the stream's underlying write function.
The buffered bytes are passed to
.BR write (2);
no data remains in the stream's user-space buffer on successful return.
.PP
For input streams associated with a seekable file,
.BR fflush ()
discards any buffered data that has been fetched from the underlying file
but not yet consumed by the application.
The stream's read position and read limit are reset so that subsequent reads
refetch from the underlying file descriptor.
.PP
The open status of the stream is unaffected.
.PP
If the
.I stream
argument is
.BR NULL ,
.BR fflush ()
walks the global list of open
.B FILE
streams maintained by the library and flushes every one of them, applying the
rules above to each stream individually according to its mode.
This is the behavior required by C99 \[char167]7.19.5.2.
If any of the per-stream flushes fails, the call returns
.B EOF
but processing of the remaining streams continues so that as many streams as
possible are drained.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Upon successful completion,
.BR fflush ()
returns 0.
Otherwise,
.B EOF
is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
When
.I stream
is
.BR NULL ,
.B EOF
is returned if any of the open streams could not be flushed; the remaining
streams are still processed.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBADF
The file descriptor underlying
.I stream
is not valid or not open for writing.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I stream
is not an open stream, or, when
.I stream
is an output stream, the buffered data could not be written.
.PP
The function may also fail and set
.I errno
for any of the errors specified for the routine
.BR write (2).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program writes a prompt without a trailing newline and
forces it out before reading a reply.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    char buf[64];

    fputs("name? ", stdout);
    if (fflush(stdout) == EOF) {
        perror("fflush");
        return 1;
    }
    if (fgets(buf, sizeof(buf), stdin) != NULL)
        fputs(buf, stdout);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR close (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR fclose (3),
.BR fopen (3),
.BR fpurge (3),
.BR setvbuf (3)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.BR fflush ()
function conforms to ISO/IEC 9899:1999 (\[lq]ISO C99\[rq]) and IEEE Std
1003.1-2008 (\[lq]POSIX.1-2008\[rq]).
The behavior of
.BR fflush ()
on input streams is an extension to the ISO C standard, specified by POSIX.
